 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend uses a local embedded SQLite index stored on each kiosk. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | Predictable lookups, no service to run, keeps all registry logic inside the component; each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives. |
+| Index storage location | Side-by-side with active snapshot file | Single directory to back up; atomic swap on activation. |
+| Index rebuild strategy | Full rebuild on each valid snapshot import | 90k records / 150 MB rebuilds well within 250 ms target on 4 GB RAM; simpler than incremental merge and avoids drift. |
+| Schema version handling | Reject unsupported versions at import; store version in index metadata | Fail fast on schema mismatch; version available for diagnostics. |
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+1. Verify the snapshot signature using the bundled Ed25519 public key.
+2. Reject if schema version is not supported (current supported: 1).
+3. Parse newline-delimited JSON records; validate each record has required fields (`serial`, `calibrationDate`, `dueDate`, `description`).
+4. Validate serial-number uniqueness within the snapshot.
+5. Build a new SQLite database in a temporary file:
+   - Create table `records(serial TEXT PRIMARY KEY, calibrationDate TEXT, dueDate TEXT, description TEXT)`
+   - Create table `metadata(key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, value TEXT)` storing `snapshotDate`, `schemaVersion`, `recordCount`
+   - Bulk-insert all records in a single transaction.
+6. Atomically replace the active database file (rename temp over active).
+7. On any failure, delete the temporary file and leave the previous active database untouched; report a detailed error (signature, schema, duplicate serial, or I/O).
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date from the active index metadata even when no matching record exists.
+
+Query: `SELECT calibrationDate, dueDate, description FROM records WHERE serial = ?` with the normalized serial. If a row exists, return `Found(record, snapshotDate)`. Otherwise return `NotFound(snapshotDate)`.
+
+## Performance Targets
+
+- Lookup latency: ≤ 250 ms p99 on 4 GB RAM kiosk hardware (includes normalization + SQLite query).
+- Snapshot import (150 MB, 90k records): complete within 5 seconds.
+- Index size: ~50 MB for 90k records (well within available storage).
+
+## Error Handling
+
+- Corrupt or missing index file on startup: treat as no active snapshot; require a valid USB import before lookups succeed.
+- I/O error during lookup: return an error result distinct from NotFound; surface to UI as "registry unavailable".
+- I/O error during import: roll back to previous index; report "import failed: <reason>".
